Things this project would like to be better. Not issues -- issues are blueprints
for building something. These are wishes, and some of them may never become
anything.

--

I would like the painting to still be beautiful with the cage and the hatching
over it. Every mark we add is a mark on somebody's artwork, and the fade rules
and the weave and the refusal to dim exist mostly out of that worry. The test is
whether a person leaves the filters off sometimes just to look.

I would like the tracing to feel like drawing rather than like data entry. It is
hours of clicking along streets, and the difference between those two experiences
is almost entirely in the feedback — whether the vertex you are about to adopt
lights up, whether the edge you are about to take shows you how much you are
taking, whether the line lands where your hand meant.

I would like somebody who has never read a word of this to work out what the cage
is within a minute of moving the mouse. No tutorial, no legend. The hairline
appears where they point, so pointing is the lesson.

I would like the game to notice when you overrule a place's default filter. The
city hands you a lens per block; disagreeing with it is you knowing something the
city's own habits do not, and that seems like exactly the kind of thing this
particular game should care about.

I would like ignorance to look good. Bare painting where you have no knowledge is
already the correct behaviour, and it would be better still if a player felt the
pull of the unhatched parts — if blindness read as invitation rather than as
absence.

I would like never to add a fifth thing to the map. Painting, cage, filters,
glow. Where-you-are wanted to be the fifth and turned out not to need to be, and
that should happen every time something new asks. The question to ask of any
candidate is whether it can be a filter.

I would like the buttons to be readable by their arrangement — for the shape of
which ones are lit to tell you what kind of place this is before you read a word.
That is a lot to ask of an icon grid and it may not survive contact with the
mechanics.

I would like the seasons, eventually, and I would like them arbitrary. A city
that changes to a random season every few months, with no cycle and no reason, is
saying something about rigid life that no amount of written setting could say as
well.
